WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.160
Hi, everyone!

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:03.620
Think of 'tell' and you think of speaking,

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:06.640
but 'tell' doesn't always mean 'tell'.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.240
Let me tell you why.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.940
OK, that time I meant speaking.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:12.820
Not only does the verb 'tell' mean

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.220
'say something to someone',

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.320
it also has a meaning similar to

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.360
'know', 'recognise', 'understand', or 'perceive'.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.960
We often use it in combination with the verb 'can'

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.940
to make 'can tell' for the present

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.390
and 'could tell' for the past.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:30.600
I can tell he's from France. He has an accent!

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:31.300
Or:

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.600
We could tell it was going to rain because of the clouds.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.060
We often use it to talk about differences.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:40.380
Then, we might use the negative or the question.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.000
Can you tell the difference between

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.180
this cup and that cup?

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.280
I can't tell the difference between this cup and that cup.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.240
We often use 'can tell' with the pronoun 'you'

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.400
to talk about something that many people should

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.000
find obvious.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.840
You can tell he's an English teacher:

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:58.440
he knows all the answers.

